! **The noncollinear GGA branch, with a derivative.** Two hydrogens with their
! moments 90 degrees apart, unevenly spaced along z, PBE, forces printed.
!
! The gradient correction of a *vector* magnetization is the one part of the
! noncollinear stack that had no measured number of any kind. Its guard was
! measured -- a bare `|m|` differentiated through its own nodes gave nan on 243
! grid components, and `safe_modulus` made them finite -- but whether the branch
! is *right* was never checked against anything: every committed PBE reference
! here is collinear or nonmagnetic, and every noncollinear one is LDA. This is the
! cell that crosses the two, and its **force** is what makes the check a
! derivative rather than an energy. The energy can be right while its derivative
! is wrong; that is the trap this project has paid for more than once.
!
! **Two atoms rather than four, and that is a measurement rather than taste.**
! `h4-noncolin-force.in`'s four moments at 90 degrees per site converge under LDA
! in 62 iterations and under PBE not at all: `pw.x` limit-cycles at an accuracy of
! 5e-6 Ry, at `mixing_beta = 0.3` after 100 iterations and again at 0.1 after 300.
! The magnetic state is frustrated there. Two sublattices at 90 degrees is still a
! genuine vector field -- `nspin_mag = 4`, three magnetization components all
! nonzero, no collinear machinery can represent it -- and it converges.
!
! `input_dft` overrides the header rather than swapping the dataset, and that is
! deliberate: `H.pz-vbc.UPF` is an LDA pseudopotential and using it under PBE is
! not a physically converged calculation, but it is the *same* inconsistency on
! both sides of the comparison and it isolates the functional. A PBE hydrogen
! dataset would change the projectors as well, and the comparison would no longer
! say which of the two moved. QE warns about the mismatch and runs.
!
! `nosym` so that the comparison is between two codes summing the same
! Brillouin-zone sample with no symmetrisation of a vector on either side, and
! conv_thr = 1e-11 for `si2-nc-force.in`'s reason: QE's printed force carries
! `force_corr`, which corrects a density that stopped short of the fixed point and
! has no counterpart in a force obtained by differentiating the energy *at* it.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
    tprnfor = .true.
    tstress = .true.
    verbosity = 'high'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 6, celldm(1) = 12.0, celldm(3) = 0.83333333333333,
    nat = 2, ntyp = 2,
    ecutwfc = 25.0,
    occupations = 'smearing', smearing = 'gaussian', degauss = 0.10
    noncolin = .true.
    input_dft = 'PBE'
    nosym = .true.
    starting_magnetization(1) = 0.6, angle1(1) = 90.0, angle2(1) =  0.0
    starting_magnetization(2) = 0.6, angle1(2) = 90.0, angle2(2) = 90.0
    nbnd = 12
 /
 &electrons
    mixing_beta = 0.3
    conv_thr = 1.0d-11
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 H1  1.008  H.pz-vbc.UPF
 H2  1.008  H.pz-vbc.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (crystal)
 H1 0.0 0.0 0.00
 H2 0.0 0.0 0.45
K_POINTS {automatic}
 1 1 2 0 0 1
